  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIn the mid/upper-levels, an Omega Block exists across the U.S., with \nthe CWA beneath the inflection point of the easternmost closed low \nover the Canadian Maritimes and the subtropical ridge centered in \nthe central Great Plains. Large-scale subsidence will persist across \nthe region through Wednesday, with the mid-level warm nose becoming \neven more-pronounced Tuesday as the center of the subtropical ridge \nsloshes eastward following the collapse of the Omega Block. High \ntemperatures for this afternoon will remain slightly above normal, \nwith highs breaching 90 degrees for some locales across the lowlands \nTuesday and Wednesday afternoon. The CWA will remain bereft of PoPs \notherwise through Wednesday afternoon, with some bouts of valley fog \nduring the predawn hours Tuesday and Wednesday amidst light winds.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nActive weather continues to be forecast late Wednesday night into \nFriday morning as a cold front approaches the region. The collapse \nof the Omega Block will have been induced by the westernmost \nshortwave trough currently digging into the Glacier Country, with \nsaid feature ejecting eastward and over the Great Lakes region by \nThursday while remaining fairly progressive. Global NWP guidance has \nbeen in agreement with the base of the trough remaining north of the \nCWA, thereby placing the CWA beneath the right-entrance region to \nthe intensifying, positively-tilted jetlets aloft. The advection of \na tall, skinny EML will be driven by modest geopotential height \nfalls, although CAPE values are forecast to be <1,500 J/kg amidst \nsubstantial cloud debris, which would limit the degree of diabatic \nheating. Orthogonally-oriented shear vectors throughout the cloud-\nbearing layer relative to the Appalachian Mountains will enhance the \npotential for upslope showers and thunderstorms Thursday, with PWATs \nclimbing between 2-3 standard deviations above the climatological \nmean. However, the deep- and cloud-layer shear vectors will also \nbecome aligned with the cold front as it attains a southerly \ncomponent to its movement by Thursday afternoon, thereby posing a \nrisk for training storms within the favorable upslope flow regime. \n\nLocalized flash flooding will be possible across portions of the CWA \nThursday, especially with any portions of the linear convection that \ncan become organized. Water-loaded cores may pose a risk for locally \nstrong wind gusts, but the severe weather risk is low Thursday. PoPs \nwill gradually wane from northwest-to-southeast late Thursday night \ninto early Friday morning as the cold front sags southward and its \naxis of dilatation becomes increasingly divergent in the wake of the \nejecting shortwave trough. That is, the passage of this cold front \nwill do little to scour out the subtropical moisture that will have \nbeen intact days prior. The cold front is forecast to stall south of \nthe CWA this weekend while continuing to decay, with off-and-on PoPs \nlasting throughout the upcoming weekend as a series of smaller-scale \nperturbations eject over the warm, and somewhat recycled, moist \nairmass as a weak surface high settles into the region. Muggy and \nwarm conditions are also forecast to continue into next weekend.",
